on march 11 2011 the fourth most  powerful earthquake in modern history  struck off the coast of eastern japan  powerful waves up to 40 meters in height  inundated coastlines and washed away  entire towns  this left the fukushima daiichi nuclear  power station  75 kilometers from the epicenter under a  dire threat the earthquake cut  electrical lines to the plant  and the tsunami flooded emergency  generators  without power to pump cooling water over  the nuclear fuel  three reactor cores melted down  gases generated from overheated fuel  exploded  and blew apart three buildings  the accident resulted in the greatest  release of radioactivity in the  environment  since the chernobyl nuclear disaster of  1986  10 years since the disaster at fukushima  cleanup at the power plant  is in its early days for one  different damage to each unit required  unique  structures to enable fuel retrieval the  structures for units 2  3 and 4 have been completed  a temporary protective enclosure  surrounds unit 1 while  engineers study how to safely remove the  fuel  major hurdles remain including figuring  out what to do with the water used to  cool melted  and re-solidified fuel debris over a  thousand massive tanks packed throughout  the campus  contain over 1.24 million tons of water  contaminated with radioactivity  a water treatment system removes  radionuclides  and reduces radioactivity  still traces of some isotopes persist  like tritium an isotope of hydrogen  tritium has a minimal impact on human  health unless ingested  and small amounts are naturally present  in seawater  and the atmosphere japan's ministry of  economy trade and industry has proposed  slowly releasing the water into the  ocean and air  but environmental groups the fishing  industry  and neighboring nations condemn this  approach  it's uncertain exactly how the discharge  of the water would impact marine life  but there are concerns about other  radioactive isotopes  of ruthenium cobalt and strontium  accumulating in fish  a solution needs to be found soon as  water storage  will likely run out in 2022  melted fuel debris has yet to be  extracted from any of the units  [Music]  but workers will try to retrieve samples  of the debris using  a remotely operated mechanical arm in  2022  as the cleanup progresses another aspect  of the nuclear accident has emerged  how the disaster impacted evacuees while  the accident was in progress  those living within 20 kilometers of the  plant  and in some adjacent highly contaminated  areas  were evacuated some places have reopened  for habitation  but about 37 000 people are still unable  to return home  uprooting thousands of people had  serious consequences for human health  among 715 residents evacuated from five  minamisoma nursing homes  the risk of death was 2.7 times higher  than before the disaster  there has also been an increase in the  lag between when women recognize  possible breast cancer symptoms  and when they see a doctor making  treatment more difficult  researchers say that better preparing  receiving facilities in the future  could help alleviate the stresses of  displacement  the lessons learned studying the  challenges of decommissioning the  fukushima daiichi power plant  and evacuation could lead to improved  responses  to future disasters  [Music]  you
 

